nsparent;"></div></figure>
<p>Therefore, <strong>we should focus on rearranging characters on the righ=
t-hand side of the string first</strong>, if possible.</p>
<p>A key insight is that the last string in a lexicographical sequence (i.e=
., the largest permutation) will always follow a <strong>non-increasing</st=
rong> order. We can see this with the string =E2=80=9Cabcc=E2=80=9D, for ex=
ample, with its largest possible permutation being =E2=80=9Cccba=E2=80=9D:<=
/p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
